# .security/zap/qa.env - settings for ZAP dynamic tests of YOUR QA app.
# Read ONLY by `warden zap qa` (the security-dast workflow), never by the normal pull-request scan.
#
# Safe to commit: NO usernames, passwords or tokens here (Warden refuses them). Those are secrets of the GitHub Environment "qa"
# (Settings > Environments > qa), or on your own machine `warden creds set NAME`. Login types and secret handling: docs/zap-authentication.md
# What is in this file decides WHAT GETS ATTACKED, so keep .security/zap/ under CODEOWNERS and review every change.
#
# Run it:  .security/warden zap qa baseline      (baseline = passive and safe | api = from an OpenAPI spec | full = ACTIVE attack scan)
# Never point this at production. Use QA or UAT with test data, with the app owner's approval.

# 1. Hosts that may be scanned (REQUIRED). Exact names or *.suffix, comma separated. The run stops before any traffic if a target is not listed.
# SEC_ZAP_ALLOWED_HOSTS=myapp-qa.example.com

# 2. What to scan. For mode full, list URLs WITH parameters, comma separated: https://myapp-qa.example.com/search?q=test,https://myapp-qa.example.com/items/1
# SEC_ZAP_URL=https://myapp-qa.example.com
# SEC_ZAP_OPENAPI=https://myapp-qa.example.com/openapi.json      # needed for mode api

# 3. Login (optional). The login TYPE and address go here; the username, password or token are secrets of the "qa" environment.
#    Types: basic | cookie | login-json | form | oauth2-client | oauth2-password   (SSO: oauth2-*)
# SEC_ZAP_AUTH=login-json
# SEC_ZAP_LOGIN_URL=https://myapp-qa.example.com/api/login
# SEC_ZAP_TOKEN_PATH=access_token

# 4. Only for an app that runs in a Docker network on the runner (not needed for a normal https address)
# SEC_ZAP_NETWORK=my_compose_net
